        "math_academy_id": "q-69623",
        "problem": "Find $G+H-(H+F)$, if\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,H=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G+H-(H+F) & =G+H-H-F \\\\  & =G+\\cancel{H}-\\cancel{H}-F \\\\  & =G-F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-F & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5-2 & 4-(-1) & -5-2 \\\\ 1-3 & -2-4 & 2-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
